## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, so your catch block and `ResetAbort()` are dead code.

### Pattern Classification

| Original Code | Pattern | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow + cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` wait | Blocking call | Graceful join with timeout |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed; just continue to let finally run
                // and the loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful exit
        if (_workerThread is not null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
            {
                // Thread didn't exit in time. In modern .NET there is no
                // way to forcibly kill it — the code MUST cooperate.
                // Log a warning; consider escalating via Process.Kill if
                // this runs uncooperative third-party code in a subprocess.
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept the token:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could unwedge it
private WorkItem GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks forever
}

// After (.NET 8) — unblocks immediately on cancellation
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---|---|
| Not passing token to blocking calls (`GetNextItem`, I/O) | `Stop()` hangs because nothing wakes the blocked thread |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter | Swallows cancellations from unrelated tokens |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive steps in `ProcessItem` |
| Replacing `Thread.Abort` with `Thread.Interrupt` | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9); use `CancellationToken` |

### If `ProcessItem` Is Long-Running

Add checkpoints inside it:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(WorkItem item, CancellationToken token)
{
    Step1(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint

    Step2(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint

    Step3(item);
}
```

### If the Code Cannot Be Modified (Uncooperative)

Move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` — this is the only reliable way to forcibly terminate uncooperative code in modern .NET.
